# Build Log — Insider Threat Detection

**Updated:** September 18, 2026
**Author:** Ryan
**AWS Account:** 021158484652 (us-east-1)

---

## What we're building

    Someone makes an API call
        -> CloudTrail records it
        -> EventBridge filters for risky ones
        -> Lambda scores it against a DynamoDB baseline
        -> SNS emails an explained alert

The point is to detect activity that is **unusual for that specific identity**,
not activity that matches a known-bad list. A stolen credential and a malicious
insider look identical in logs, so we baseline per-identity behaviour instead.

## Status

| Phase | What | Done |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Terraform state backend (S3) | Yes |
| 2 | CloudTrail + log bucket | Yes |
| 3 | DynamoDB baseline table + seed data | Yes |
| 4 | EventBridge rule + Lambda | Yes |
| 6 | SNS email alerting | Yes |
| **5** | **Scoring logic + explanations** | **Not started** |
| 7 | MITRE mapping, attack demo, README | Not started |

**Phase 5 is the actual project.** Right now the Lambda alerts on every
mutating call and never reads the baseline table. It is a louder version of
the tools we said we'd improve on. The scoring is what makes it ours.

---

## Part 1 — Local setup

Each person does this once, on their own machine. Everyone needs their own IAM
user and their own access key. Do not share credentials: CloudTrail attributes
every event to an identity, and sharing collapses the baselines that the whole
project depends on.

### Install the tools (Windows, PowerShell — not as Administrator)

    winget install HashiCorp.Terraform
    winget install Amazon.AWSCLI

Close PowerShell and reopen it so PATH refreshes, then check:

    terraform -version     # we are on 1.16
    aws --version

### Get an access key

1. IAM -> Users -> your user -> Security credentials
2. Create access key -> **Command Line Interface (CLI)**
3. The secret shows once only. Do not paste it anywhere.

### Configure and verify

    aws configure
    # key id, secret, us-east-1, json

    aws sts get-caller-identity

The ARN must end in `:user/your-name`. **If it says `:root`, stop** — delete
that key and make an IAM user instead. Root cannot be restricted by any policy
and can close the account.

### Credential rules

- Never create a root access key
- Never share keys between teammates
- Never commit a key, and never paste one into chat or Discord
- Delete your key at the end of the semester
- Enable MFA on your IAM user

