## Part 5 — Phase 4: EventBridge + Lambda

### What we learned from a real CloudTrail record

Before writing the Lambda we pulled a log file and looked at it. Four things
that shaped the code:

1. **`userIdentity.type` varies** — `IAMUser`, `AssumedRole`, `AWSService`.
   The `AWSService` records have no `arn` and no `accessKeyId`, so naive field
   access crashes. For `AssumedRole` the real principal is at
   `sessionContext.sessionIssuer.arn`, not `arn`.
2. **`sourceIPAddress` isn't always an IP** — service calls put a hostname
   there, like `resource-explorer-2.amazonaws.com`.
3. **`readOnly: false`** is the best volume filter. We only care about
   mutating calls.
4. **`invokedBy` exists on service-initiated calls** and is absent on human
   ones. Cleanest way to drop AWS's own background noise.

### `lambda/handler.py` (stub version)

```python
import json

def resolve_identity(ui):
    t = ui.get("type")
    if t == "IAMUser":
        return ui.get("arn")
    if t == "AssumedRole":
        return ui.get("sessionContext", {}).get("sessionIssuer", {}).get("arn")
    return None

def handler(event, context):
    d = event.get("detail", {})
    ui = d.get("userIdentity", {})
    summary = {
        "identity": resolve_identity(ui),
        "identity_type": ui.get("type"),
        "access_key": ui.get("accessKeyId"),
        "api": "{}:{}".format(d.get("eventSource"), d.get("eventName")),
        "region": d.get("awsRegion"),
        "time": d.get("eventTime"),
        "source_ip": d.get("sourceIPAddress"),
        "read_only": d.get("readOnly"),
    }
    print(json.dumps(summary))
    return {"ok": True}
```

### `main/eventbridge.tf`

```hcl
data "archive_file" "detector" {
  type        = "zip"
  source_file = "${path.module}/../lambda/handler.py"
  output_path = "${path.module}/detector.zip"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "detector" {
  name = "insider-threat-detector"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect    = "Allow"
      Principal = { Service = "lambda.amazonaws.com" }
      Action    = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "detector" {
  name              = "/aws/lambda/insider-threat-detector"
  retention_in_days = 7
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "detector" {
  role = aws_iam_role.detector.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = ["logs:CreateLogStream", "logs:PutLogEvents"]
        Resource = "${aws_cloudwatch_log_group.detector.arn}:*"
      },
      {
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = ["dynamodb:GetItem", "dynamodb:Query"]
        Resource = aws_dynamodb_table.baseline.arn
      },
      {
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Action   = ["sns:Publish"]
        Resource = aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_lambda_function" "detector" {
  function_name    = "insider-threat-detector"
  role             = aws_iam_role.detector.arn
  handler          = "handler.handler"
  runtime          = "python3.13"
  filename         = data.archive_file.detector.output_path
  source_code_hash = data.archive_file.detector.output_base64sha256
  timeout          = 10

  environment {
    variables = {
      BASELINE_TABLE = aws_dynamodb_table.baseline.name
      ALERT_TOPIC    = aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn
    }
  }

  depends_on = [aws_cloudwatch_log_group.detector]
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_event_rule" "risky" {
  name = "insider-threat-risky-calls"

  event_pattern = jsonencode({
    "detail-type" = ["AWS API Call via CloudTrail"]
    detail = {
      readOnly    = [false]
      eventSource = [{ "anything-but" = ["logs.amazonaws.com"] }]
      userIdentity = {
        invokedBy = [{ exists = false }]
        type      = ["IAMUser", "AssumedRole"]
      }
    }
  })
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_event_target" "detector" {
  rule = aws_cloudwatch_event_rule.risky.name
  arn  = aws_lambda_function.detector.arn
}

resource "aws_lambda_permission" "events" {
  statement_id  = "AllowEventBridge"
  action        = "lambda:InvokeFunction"
  function_name = aws_lambda_function.detector.function_name
  principal     = "events.amazonaws.com"
  source_arn    = aws_cloudwatch_event_rule.risky.arn
}
```

    terraform init      # archive provider is new
    terraform apply

### The feedback loop (important)

Our first version had a bug worth knowing about. The Lambda writes to
CloudWatch Logs. `CreateLogStream` is a mutating call. EventBridge matched it
and invoked the Lambda again — which wrote another log.

The `anything-but: logs.amazonaws.com` clause fixes it. Without it, a runaway
Lambda loop is one of the only ways this project could actually cost money.

### Test

```powershell
aws s3api create-bucket --bucket test-detect-021158484652
aws s3api delete-bucket --bucket test-detect-021158484652
aws logs tail /aws/lambda/insider-threat-detector --since 2m --filter-pattern "identity"
```

Two lines, both `admin.me`, and **no** `logs.amazonaws.com:CreateLogStream`.
Measured latency from API call to Lambda: **about 9 seconds.**

